+(duration)=
+
+### Duration
+
+The {class}`Duration` type converts a duration into a {class}`datetime.timedelta`. A duration is either a whole number of
+seconds, like `120`, or one or more groups of an integer followed by a unit: `d` for days, `h` for hours, `m` for
+minutes, and `s` for seconds. Each unit may appear at most once and in that order, without spaces, so `2d`, `1h30m`,
+`90m`, and `1d2h3m4s` are all valid. Units are case-insensitive. Negative and fractional values are rejected.
+
+```{eval-rst}
+.. click:example::
+
+    @click.command()
+    @click.option("--timeout", type=click.Duration())
+    def wait(timeout):
+        click.echo(f"Waiting {timeout.total_seconds():g} seconds.")
+
+.. click:run::
+
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=1h30m'])
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=90'])
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=1.5h'])
+```
+
